[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      4[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stdout, byte-identical stderr, and the same exit status for every input.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      5[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      6[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## How it was verified
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      7[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      8[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Command | Purpose |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `cd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && cmake --build .` | builds the reference, `c_src/build/driver` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `cd translation && cargo build --release` | builds `translation/target/release/driver` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `cd translation && cargo test` | runs the differential suite (both profiles pass) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Both programs are driven as subprocesses over a pipe — never linked as a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m library — because that is how the comparison is made.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Evidence gathered:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **47 tests** in `tests/differential.rs` (39, enumerated branches) and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `tests/fuzz_differential.rs` (8, randomized + process-level). Every test
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   asserts all three of stdout, stderr and exit status. None is `#[ignore]`d,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   skipped or disabled.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **~17,000 distinct inputs** total: exhaustive sweeps over `(length, rotate
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   amount)`, `(length, split position)`, `(len1, len2)` merge/interleave pairs,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   all 256 byte values, all lengths `0..=256`, plus ~4,800 randomized inputs
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (structured, unstructured, and raw byte soup including NUL and controls).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **Branch coverage of the C.** The corpus was replayed through a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `gcc --coverage` build of `main.c` (compiled from a copy in `/tmp`; `c_src`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   was not touched). Result: **0 unexecuted lines and 0 one-sided branches in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   reachable code.** Every line gcov reports as unexecuted is provably
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   unreachable from `main` — see the inventory at the bottom.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatch found
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. Exit status on a closed stdout (SIGPIPE disposition)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** With a reader that closes the pipe before the program finishes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m writing, the two programs disagreed on how they terminated:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ ./c_src/build/driver < big_input | (read -r _; exit 0)   # C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 141                       # killed by SIGPIPE (128 + 13)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ ./translation/target/release/driver < big_input | (read -r _; exit 0)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0                         # exited normally
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Reproducing this needs an input whose stdout exceeds the pipe capacity, e.g.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 100 buffers of 256 bytes (`op=1`, ~91.8 KB of output).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** Not a translation error in the logic — a difference in process
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m startup. The Rust standard library sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m runs, so a write to a closed pipe returns `EPIPE` instead of raising a signal.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `Out::flush` discards write errors (`let _ = ...`), exactly as C's implicit
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `exit`-time flush does, so the failure vanished and the process exited with the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m normal status. A C program starts with `SIGPIPE` at `SIG_DFL`, so the same write
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m kills it with signal 13.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `restore_default_sigpipe()` in `src/main.rs`, called first thing in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main`, resets the handler to `SIG_DFL` via a bare `extern "C" { fn signal(...) }`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m declaration (no new dependency). It is a no-op whenever stdout stays open, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m nothing else changes. Regression-tested by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `closed_stdout_matches_sigpipe_disposition`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviors that look like bugs and were confirmed faithful
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These were the highest-risk spots. Each is reproduced deliberately, was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m suspected of being wrong, and was confirmed identical by direct comparison. They
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m are recorded so the next reader does not "fix" them.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### `scanf("%d")` saturates as `long`, then truncates to `int`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m glibc converts the digit run as a `long`, clamping to `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN` on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m overflow, and only then stores the low 32 bits. The visible results are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m surprising and both programs agree on all of them:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Input | Parsed as `int` | Observable effect |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- | --- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `1 99999999999999999999` | `-1` | `Error: Invalid buffer count -1` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `1 -99999999999999999999` | `0` | `Error: Invalid buffer count 0` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `1 2147483648` | `-2147483648` | `Error: Invalid buffer count -2147483648` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `4294967296 1 0` | `0` | runs `OP_COPY`, not "unknown operation" |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `4294967297 1 0` | `1` | runs `OP_REVERSE` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Boundaries `9223372036854775807/8` and `-9223372036854775808/9` were checked
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m individually, as were 5,000-digit runs and 5,000 leading zeros. Covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `scanf_overflow_saturates_then_truncates` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `scanf_int_truncation_boundaries`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### A negative split position becomes an enormous `size_t`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main` reads `split_pos` as `int` and passes it to a `size_t` parameter, so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m value sign-extends *before* `buffer_split`'s `split_pos > src->length` check:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m input:  3 1 3 1 2 3 -1
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stderr: Error: Split position 18446744073709551615 exceeds length 3
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m input:  3 1 3 1 2 3 -2147483648
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stderr: Error: Split position 18446744071562067968 exceeds length 3
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The Rust reproduces this with `split_pos as i64 as usize` (not `as usize`, which
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m would widen from `i32` differently). Covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `op_split_negative_position_becomes_huge`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### `buffer_rotate` checks `positions == 0` before normalizing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The early return tests the *raw* amount, so `positions == length` skips the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m early return and performs a zero-length rotate — same output, different path.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C's `%` truncates toward zero, so negatives need the `+= length` correction.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `i32::MIN` is safe here only because `length` is always positive.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `op_rotate_all_amounts` sweeps every amount in `±(2·length+2)` for lengths
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1, 2, 3, 5, 8 and 256, plus `INT_MIN`/`INT_MAX`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### `OP_INTERLEAVE`'s error message has no format specifier
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `Error: Interleaved length exceeds maximum` prints no length, unlike
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `Error: Merged length %zu exceeds maximum` right above it. Asymmetric, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m correct. Covered by `op_interleave_length_boundary`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### Validation happens after all input is consumed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m An unknown operation is only diagnosed at the bottom of `main`, so buffer errors
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m surface first: `77 1 -5` reports `Error: Invalid buffer length -5`, not
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `Error: Unknown operation 77`. Covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `unknown_operation_still_reads_all_buffers_first`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### `%d` crosses newlines
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Line structure is irrelevant to the whole program; `\n`, `\r\n`, `\t`, `\x0b`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and `\x0c` are all just separators. Verified with a full `isspace` alphabet, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m by feeding every maximum-size input twice, once space-separated and once
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m newline-separated (`maximum_size_inputs_for_every_operation`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### Uninitialized destination buffers are observationally zeroed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `buffer_t temp;` / `buffer_t merged;` / `buffer_t part1, part2;` are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m uninitialized in the C, and `init_buffer_array` uses `malloc`, not `calloc`.
